The hardware may have interrupt-on-change enabled on some pins from a previous configuration (e.g., after a reboot). Without explicitly writing 0 to the `MCP_GPINTEN` register before setting up the interrupt controller, the chip retains those enabled interrupts. When an interrupt fires for a pin without a registered nested handler, `mcp23s08_irq` tries to call `handle_nested_irq` with an invalid pointer, causing a null pointer dereference and kernel crash.

The code statements that fix the bug are:

```c
		ret = mcp_write(mcp, MCP_GPINTEN, 0);
		if (ret < 0)
			return dev_err_probe(dev, ret, "can't disable interrupts\n");

```

Reason:
This writes 0 to the `MCP_GPINTEN` register, disabling all pin interrupts on the chip. It ensures that no stale interrupt configuration from the hardware’s previous state can trigger the interrupt handler before nested handlers are properly registered. This prevents the null pointer dereference that occurred when a pin with an enabled interrupt lacked a registered handler.

